<p>Fresh onion and dehydrated onion come from the same Indian fields, but at the port they are two separate products with separate customs codes, separate policy histories and separate logistics. That difference matters most to buyers who were caught out when India restricted fresh-onion exports and are asking whether a dried line would have kept their supply moving. This guide is for food manufacturers, seasoning blenders and importers weighing the two forms. It covers how the export controls on fresh onion have worked since 2020, why the dried heading has stayed outside them, which dehydrated forms stand in for which fresh uses, and where a switch between the two goes wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p><strong>What the dehydrated series shows.</strong> India's dehydrated onion exports were flat for three years: USD 158.9 million in 2021, USD 167.8 million in 2022 and USD 167.4 million in 2023. They then rose to USD 221.4 million in 2024, about a third higher, and volume rose from about 90,404 MT to 113,241 MT. Fresh-onion exports were prohibited for part of that period, from December 2023 to May 2024. Some buyers may have moved volume from fresh to dried while the ban was on, but the customs data can't prove it, and demand from food processors in the United States, Brazil and Europe grew too. Read the 2024 jump as consistent with a switch, not as evidence of one.</p>

<h2>Two customs headings, two policy tracks</h2>
<p>India's export policy is attached to the tariff line, not to the plant the product came from. Fresh or chilled onion falls under HS 0703.10. Dried onion, whole, cut, sliced, broken or in powder, falls under HS 0712.20. The government has three tools for restricting an export, and fresh onion has seen all three:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Minimum Export Price (MEP).</strong> A floor, set on an FOB basis, below which a consignment may not be exported. It stops low-priced export contracts from draining domestic supply.</li>
  <li><strong>Export duty.</strong> A percentage charged on the export value, notified through customs. It raises the landed cost abroad without banning trade.</li>
  <li><strong>Prohibition.</strong> DGFT amends the export policy of the tariff line from "Free" to "Prohibited", sometimes with exceptions for government-to-government supplies or cargo already loading.</li>
</ul>
<p>Each of these tools is written against the fresh-onion line. Dehydrated onion is Free for export under DGFT's ITC(HS) schedule, and it has not shared fresh onion's history of MEPs and prohibitions. That is why food manufacturers treat it as the more plannable product, even though both lines draw on the same crop.</p>

<h2>A timeline of fresh-onion export controls since 2020</h2>
<p>The government's own releases show how often the fresh-onion position has changed:</p>
