<p>The reason is domestic supply. The government's March 2025 release describes the controls as measures "to ensure domestic availability" after lower production and high international prices from August 2023. It also notes that rabi onion makes up 70-75% of India's onion production, so the size of the spring crop largely decides how much room there is to export fresh onion for the rest of the year. Even under restriction, fresh-onion exports did not stop entirely: the release puts them at 17.17 lakh MT in FY 2023-24.</p>
<p>The list above ends at the last change we have verified from a primary source. Fresh-onion policy can move again with a single notification, so the current position has to be confirmed when you place an order. We check the DGFT and customs position for HS 0703.10 before every fresh-onion quote.</p>

<h2>Which dehydrated forms replace which fresh uses</h2>
<p>If you are moving some volume from fresh to dried, start from what the fresh onion does in your product, not from the list of forms:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Diced fresh onion in soups, ready meals and sauces</strong> maps to kibbled or chopped pieces, which rehydrate in the cooking liquid and stay visible.</li>
  <li><strong>Finely chopped onion in meat products, dips and dressings</strong> maps to minced onion, which spreads evenly and doses well through feeders.</li>
  <li><strong>Onion cooked down into a base or paste</strong> maps to granules or powder, which disperse fully and give flavour without pieces.</li>
  <li><strong>Raw onion for salads, garnish and retail sale</strong> has no dried equivalent. Keep that volume on fresh supply.</li>
</ul>
<p>Two specification points change when you make the move. Moisture is the first: a dried line is held at ≤5–6%, and the contract needs to protect that figure through transit, because dried onion takes up water from humid air. Microbiology is the second. A fresh onion is cooked in your plant; dried onion added to a product with no further heat step needs a written microbiological panel. Particle size for granules and powder should be set as a sieve range with a tolerance, agreed against a sample.</p>

<h2>Logistics: dry freight against ventilated produce</h2>
<p>Fresh onion is a living bulb. It ships in ventilated containers that manage airflow, keeps 2-4 months if properly cured, and loses weight and quality the longer it waits at either end. It sells by the season, and a delayed vessel or a policy change during the season can leave a buyer without stock.</p>
<p>Dehydrated onion ships in a standard dry container and keeps 18-24 months in cool, dry, sealed storage, so it can be bought ahead and held. The product is bulky rather than heavy: expect about 12-14 MT in a 20ft container and 14-16 MT in a 40ft, depending on form. Several kilograms of fresh onion go into each kilogram of dried product, so a container of dried onion carries far more onion-equivalent than its weight suggests, and there is no water to pay freight on.</p>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>For onion, the HS code on the shipping documents decides which policy the shipment is assessed under, so it has to match the goods exactly. Dehydrated onion should be declared under 0712.20 on the invoice, the packing list and the phytosanitary certificate, with the form (kibbled, minced, powder and so on) stated. A fresh-onion shipment needs a second check at the time of booking, because an MEP, duty or prohibition in force on the shipping date applies to it regardless of when the contract was signed. Share your compliance checklist and we will confirm the documentation for your shipment.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>How fresh-onion controls reach dehydrated onion prices</h2>
<p>Dehydrated onion is not subject to the fresh-onion MEP or duty, but it is not immune to them either. The dried product is made from fresh onion, and export controls act on the domestic fresh market that dehydration plants buy from.</p>
<p>The effect can run both ways. When fresh-onion export is restricted, more onion stays in India and domestic prices tend to soften, which can ease the raw-material cost of dehydration. When the reason for the restriction is a short crop, as in 2023, raw onion is expensive anyway, and dried onion prices rise with it. Watching the rabi harvest tells you more about dehydrated pricing than watching the MEP.</p>
<p>Other factors sit on top of the crop. Processing plants buy most of their raw onion during the harvest campaign, so contracts placed then are priced on known costs, and later contracts depend on stocks held. Finer forms need more milling and sieving. A wider microbiology panel adds testing. Freight and the rupee-dollar rate move the landed figure. Fresh-onion offers carry one more variable: a policy change between contract and loading can alter the cost of the shipment or stop it.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your form, mesh, moisture and microbiology panel, packaging and destination port. For fresh onion, the quote is confirmed against the export policy in force on the day.</p>
