<h2>What goes wrong when buyers switch between fresh and dehydrated</h2>
<p><strong>Switching in the middle of a restriction.</strong> The worst time to qualify a dried supplier is the week fresh exports stop. Every buyer with the same idea is asking for samples at once, your own customers still have to sign off the new ingredient, and your plant may need new dosing equipment. A buyer who has already approved a dried line can simply move volume across when fresh supply tightens. Qualify a dehydrated supplier now, even if fresh stays your main source.</p>
<p><strong>Converting quantities by weight.</strong> A recipe written for a given weight of fresh onion does not need the same weight of dried onion. Work out the dried dose from trials in your own product, then set the purchase quantity from that, or the first order will be several times too large.</p>
<p><strong>Keeping a fresh-onion hygiene assumption.</strong> Fresh onion is peeled and cooked in the buyer's plant, so the microbiology on arrival matters less. Dried onion is often added straight into a product with no further heat step. If the purchase order has no microbiological limits, the lot is tested against the supplier's defaults, which may not match your finished-product specification.</p>
<p><strong>Assuming dried onion arrives dry.</strong> A lot packed at the right moisture can arrive damp and lumped if liners are torn or warm product is loaded into a cold container. Check the bags nearest the doors and the top layers first.</p>
<p><strong>Signing a long fresh-onion contract with no policy clause.</strong> A fresh-onion contract that runs for months should say what happens if an MEP, a duty or a prohibition is introduced before loading: whether price is renegotiated, the balance is cancelled, or volume moves to dried onion.</p>
<p><strong>Declaring the wrong code.</strong> A dried product described loosely, or a fresh consignment under the dried code, will be caught at customs and delays both.</p>
<!-- operator-detail:end -->

